A||N||16||Eric Marsh||Maximum Rubber?||||5
Z||000000||Eric Marsh||10-22-1999||03:27 PM||||I'm starting to look at new wheels and rubber for my '88 GT. At this point I'm figuring I'll probably be using a 17" wheel. With the big V8 I'm putting in I need lots of rubber on the back, and to compensate for the car's tendency to push I'm also looking to fit as much rubber as I can up there.<P>I don't mind if the tires and the back tires in particular extend beyond the wheel wells. It doesn't rain too often here so water being thrown off the tires isn't much of an issue. The car has been lowered and I guess that means it may be necessary to re-radius the wheel wells for the bigger rubber. That doesn't bother me, as I'm planning to paint anyway.<P>Currently I'm thinking of using the Pirelli P7000 Super Sports, which are supposed to be a good compromise of stick and life. I want at least a 225 up front and a 245 in back. But frankly I wouldn't mind going beyond that to something like a 265 or more in back and whatever the front will take.<P>Has anyone got any experience with putting maximum rubber on an '88? I'm curious about wheel size, offsets and any other related info.<P>Thanks,<P>Eric<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||10-23-1999||04:35 AM||||I've never gone beyond 225 in the back so I can't help you there. Anyone else perhaps?||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-23-1999||07:30 AM||||There are several tire size treads on the Forum. I don't have any experience with the 88 models or 17 inch wheels, but the older Fieros will take 245-60-15 tires on the rear with no problem.<P>With a V8 sitting in there, it might squat down the back more than normal. This might affect clearance slightly. You may have to experiment using some old tires first or find a tire shop that'll work with you and mount up a couple different sizes to try.<P>Check with V8 Archie and see if he knows what some of his customers are using for tire sizes. I personally would not cut out the wheel well||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||10-23-1999||08:01 AM||||I run BFG 245/60-15's on back of my Formula. No problems at all so far (I dont put alot of miles on her though).  My brief experience with these tires would lead me to conclude that for maximum straight line performance on a high hp V8 Fiero, one would want something a bit beefier back there. (ie.. My little 2.8 can still break em' loose quite easily).<P>Mike<p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 10-23-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||10-23-1999||12:32 PM||||Nearly every car I've seen with a set of Pirelli tires has had a de-lamination problem on at least one tire before the tires are used up.<BR>I've heard 245 is the limit,but I suppose you could use a different offset,or spacers and push that a little.Or go outwards,maybe by using a wider rim and the 245 section tire you could create a sidewall profile that would still fit(barely)and imrove the stance.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||10-23-1999||06:49 PM||||<BR><B>lowCG</B>, you mentioned "<B>spacers</B>". The use of spacers between the wheels and rotors has been (very) briefly discussed in other threads. I remember that <B>batboy</B> didn't recommend them. He felt that there was a chance that they could allow the wheel lugs to work their way loose, etc. I value batboy's opinion, but do you (or anyone else) have any comments on the use of spacers that could convince me otherwise? I'm interested because I have limited inner clearance between my wheels/tires and the struts/knuckles. I really don't want to change the wheels if using spacers will accomplish what I need. However, I also don't want the wheels falling off the car. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000006||fastonpumpgas||10-24-1999||09:05 AM||||yeah I think that a car is made to a specific off set that it has to stay on.  I have alot of friends that "skateboarded" truck and regals, and what happens when you use spacers the the flange that the spindle is mount on starts to bend and the toe in gets terrible, which we all know what that cause. tick-tock-tick-tock . Uneven tire wear.  I have seen it to the point on a new yorker that the car was lower and the the wheels were bowed.  I wouldnt recomment it for a long time use.  I think one of the reasons spacers were made is so you can run wider tires on your street/strip car on the weekends.  but I dont think a 10 inch rim would stick out that far. I dont know about 17's though, might bottom ou tin the wheel well????<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||10-24-1999||02:12 PM||||I value anyone's opinion that has actual experience and can explain exactly why something is(or isn't).That's how a good knowledge base is formed for easier decisions in the future,maybe I should have defined the spacers a little more clearly,and,especially since these cars load the wheel/bearings more than other cars,it's important to take all cautions in this area;I would only use either a 1/16" or less plain shim to allow for a wheel to fit,or get some spacers that locate on the register that's on the hub(where the stock wheels fit snug,in the middle)to keep the load off of the studs.With longer studs,it's normal to go up to 3/4" this way.<BR>I may be playing around with some custom spacers to get my new wheels to locate on the hub in this way when I put on my new 11.25" rotors,because they don't the way they came,and I can't say for sure,but it looks like Batboy's Grand Am brakes lose the hub locating feature as well,just like a not-so-good spacer,or aftermarket wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Patrick||10-24-1999||04:06 PM||||<BR>Hey guys, thanks for the feedback on <B>spacers</B>.<P>I understand what <B>fastonpumpgas</B> is saying about the added stress possibly causing things to bend. My question would be though, how much of an offset has been added? What is the thickness of the spacers in these problematic applications? It's possible that this worst case scenario is only achieved through the use of extremely wide spacers. I'm only interested myself in using a spacer maybe a <B>half inch</B> thick.<P>So I'd also like to ask <B>lowCG</B>, when a spacer of ANY thickness is used, does this always mean that the wheel studs need to be replaced with ones that are longer? For example, if I was to use a spacer a quarter of an inch thick, would I necessarily need to use studs that are a quarter of an inch longer than stock, or could I simply retain the original wheel studs?||||reg||5
Z||000009||lowCG||10-25-1999||12:42 AM||||Whenever you hang something out there it's going to stress stuff,but,when your wheels/tires are wide,where the middle of the tire is only an inch,say,from the original factory position,the load on the wheel bearings/spindle is going to be way less than the skateboard/superfly stuff that's up to six inches out,but with bicycle sized rubber.<BR>Another thing that occurs when the track is increased is that the working spring rates are affected(softened)because of the increase in the "arm" or mechanical advantage(leverage)yet the handling nearly always improves with width.<BR>Patrick,yes,a half inch is a very safe spacer size as far as spindle/wheel bearings are concerned,and you would need some longer studs,the 54mm ones used in the Grand Am brake swap are the ones you'd want for half inch spacers,but do try and get something that fits both the hub,and the wheel center,or it could create a mess for you where you constantly are retorqeing the lug bolts.<BR>The lug bolts aren't really meant to take axial loads;the registers,or stepped part on the hubs are.<BR>Glad this came up,I hadn't noticed my wheel/hub slop until now,and will torque my wheels much more carefully until I make some little spacer deals to correctly fit both parts,or better yet,machine the new rotors  to hold the wheel,if the rotors first fit the hub that is...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||10-25-1999||07:49 AM||||I still think there are better ways get the correct off-set on your car than to use spacers, like using different wheels. My brother used some aluminum spacers on his old Chevy years ago, so he could mount a pair of 10 inch wide rims on the back with big meats. He was constantly having to retorque them. The lug nuts seemed to periodically loosen up. If you do use them, make sure to retorque the lugs during oil changes or some other regular service interval. By the way Patrick, did you ever replace those nearly bald tires? Here is a website I stumbled by lately if you are serious about using spacers.<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/wheel/wheel.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/wheel/wheel.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Fiero||10-25-1999||10:46 AM||||Here we go again...You can shove up to 9 inch rim on an '88 Fiero and a 17 inch would fit... I wouldn't recommend doing a spacer thicker than 1/16... if you want to make the wheel fit right is go with coils over that way you don't have to worry about hitting the knuckles on your struts. I have an '88 Formula and I have 17's all around... I just got my new 17 inch wheels.. 17X7 1/2 in the fronts althought I can go 17X8's and the rears are 17X8.5's and again I can go up to 17X9's. Going on a 10 inch will not fit and you are pushing your limits.. One thing to remember. The '88 Fiero is the same offsets as the other Fiero models...'84 -'87. They have 35MM offsets. The fronts are more of 45MMoffsets on the '88's while the '84's -'87's remains 35MM offsets...Another to consider is that in order to get a proper offset that would fit the rears and fronts of the '88's is you have to buy either a 2-piece wheel or a 3-piece wheels...Question is..Can you afford to spend that much money??? There are some 1-piece wheels in the market that have the width that you want along with the bolt pattern for out cars...But it's very hard to find one. Most common width size on a 17 is 17X7 1/2.. in which you can still shove 245/45/17 or 245/40/17 and not to worry about hitting the knuckle of the struts.. I know this because some guys in my Fiero Clubs have this set-ups.. Unfortunately for my taste..I'll invest with a coils over and go with my 17X8.5 inch wide.. Reason,One... it looks really good from the rear of the car with that wide tires. Two..it makes your car look aggressive.. Anyway...I hope this helps..By the way..I am one of those people that got lucky and found a 1-piece 17 inch wheels that would fit on my car.. Although the fronts have to be shave down to 42MM from 35MM.. The rears are 40MM offsets and I am paying only $600.00 for all four and not even charge to pay taxes.. The down side is that I have waited for almost 3 months to get this, because it's coming from Italy... And I am picking them up this week..Thank God!!!I guess it's well worth, because they look like Ferrari wheels and it will look good on my cars set-up....||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eric Marsh||10-25-1999||03:33 PM||||To 88 Fiero:<P>Those wheels you mentioned sound interesting. I can afford to wait if they are the right choice. Can you provide a picture of them or give some additional info, such as brand, model, vendor, etc.? Also, do you have any idea how much they weigh?<P>Thanks,<P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000013||Patrick||10-25-1999||05:59 PM||||<BR>batboy, you're asking about my tires. Well, I.... umm.... I.... ahhh.... but.....<P>No.... I haven't bought them yet. [[ Hanging head in shame. ]]<P>If it's any consolation, I really do respect wet pavement. I'm careful, but I realize that's no excuse. Thanks for the info on the site regarding <B>spacers</B>. I just hate to buy new wheels because I need a half inch or so of extra clearance. <P>Call me cheap.... (just don't call me collect)....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000014||batboy||10-25-1999||08:34 PM||||Patrick, been there and done that! Guess I should be thankful I have a good paying job now. I sure didn't always have it so nice though.<P>You've run those tires you got on right now long enough to wear them out. If they haven't rubbed yet, they ain't gonna. As long as you put something close to the some size back on, you'll be ok.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Patrick||10-25-1999||08:53 PM||||<BR>That's the problem batboy, I don't want to put the same tire size back on. The car came with <B>205/60/14</B> tires on it all around and I've always disliked how they've looked in the back. They're way too puny. I would like to put <B>235/60/14</B> tires on in the back. Actually, I would like to use wider tires, but I think even <B>235/60/14</B> is pushing it on a six inch wide rim.||||reg||9
Z||000016||batboy||10-25-1999||09:54 PM||||You might try 215-60-14 in the front and 235-60-14 in the back. I have that on my 85 GT right now and I have all kinds of room. Maybe you won't need a spacer with those wheels.||||reg||1
